{"task_id": "Mbpp/260", "solution": "import math\n\ndef newman_prime(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the nth Newman-Shanks-Williams prime number,\n    where n is the index in the NSW sequence.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _get_nsw_number(k: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the k-th Newman-Shanks-Williams number S_k.\"\"\"\n        if k == 0:\n            return 1\n        if k == 1:\n            return 1\n        \n        s_prev2 = 1  # Corresponds to S_0\n        s_prev1 = 1  # Corresponds to S_1\n        \n        for i in range(2, k + 1):\n            current_s = 2 * s_prev1 + s_prev2\n            s_prev2 = s_prev1\n            s_prev1 = current_s\n            \n        return s_prev1\n\n    def _is_prime(number: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a given number is prime.\"\"\"\n        if number < 2:\n            return False\n        if number == 2:\n            return True\n        if number % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n        \n        for i in range(3, int(math.sqrt(number)) + 1, 2):\n            if number % i == 0:\n                return False\n                \n        return True\n\n    # Calculate the n-th Newman-Shanks-Williams number.\n    nsw_number = _get_nsw_number(n)\n    \n    # As per design, a primality check is included.\n    # The problem implies that for valid 'n', the calculated NSW number will be prime.\n    if _is_prime(nsw_number):\n        return nsw_number\n    # If the number is not prime, it means the input 'n' was unexpected\n    # according to the problem's premise to return a prime number.\n    # Following constraints, no exception is raised; the function returns the number.\n    return nsw_number"}
