{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets the leftmost unset bit in the binary representation of the input number.\n    This means finding the most significant bit that is 0 within or immediately\n    to the left of the number's current significant bits, and setting it to 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    k = number.bit_length()\n    # Iterate from the most significant bit down to the least significant bit\n    # within the number's current representation.\n    for i in range(k - 1, -1, -1):\n        # Check if the i-th bit is 0\n        if not (number & (1 << i)):\n            # If unset, set this bit and return the new number\n            return number | (1 << i)\n    \n    # If all bits from 0 to k-1 are set (e.g., number is 0, 1, 3, 7, etc.),\n    # the leftmost unset bit is at position k.\n    # For number = 0, k = 0, so 0 | (1 << 0) = 1.\n    # For number = 7 (0b111), k = 3, so 7 | (1 << 3) = 7 | 8 = 15 (0b1111).\n    return number | (1 << k)"}
