{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def max_product(array: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum product formed by multiplying numbers of an increasing subsequence of the array.\n    Uses dynamic programming with O(n^2) time complexity to consider all valid subsequences.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(array)\n\n    # Handle edge cases as specified in requirements\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return array[0]\n\n    # dp_max_prod[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending with array[i].\n    # dp_min_prod[i] stores the minimum product of an increasing subsequence ending with array[i].\n    # The min product is needed to correctly handle negative numbers, as multiplying a small\n    # negative number by another negative number can yield a large positive product.\n    dp_max_prod = [0] * n\n    dp_min_prod = [0] * n\n\n    # Initialize dp arrays. Each element itself is an increasing subsequence of length 1.\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp_max_prod[i] = array[i]\n        dp_min_prod[i] = array[i]\n\n    # Initialize max_overall_product. We start with negative infinity to ensure that\n    # any valid product (even a single negative number if it's the max) is captured.\n    max_overall_product = float('-inf')\n\n    # After initializing dp arrays with single element products, update max_overall_product\n    # to include the maximum of these initial single-element products.\n    for val in array:\n        max_overall_product = max(max_overall_product, val)\n\n    # Fill dp arrays for subsequences of length > 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            # If array[i] is greater than array[j], it can extend an increasing subsequence\n            # that ends at array[j].\n            if array[i] > array[j]:\n                # Calculate two potential products:\n                # 1. Extending a max product subsequence ending at j\n                # 2. Extending a min product subsequence ending at j (crucial for negative numbers)\n                prod1 = dp_max_prod[j] * array[i]\n                prod2 = dp_min_prod[j] * array[i]\n\n                # Update dp_max_prod[i]: it's the maximum of its current value (from length-1 subseq or earlier j)\n                # and the new potential products.\n                dp_max_prod[i] = max(dp_max_prod[i], prod1, prod2)\n                # Update dp_min_prod[i]: similarly, for the minimum.\n                dp_min_prod[i] = min(dp_min_prod[i], prod1, prod2)\n        \n        # After considering all previous elements array[j] to extend an increasing subsequence\n        # ending at array[i], update the overall maximum product found so far.\n        max_overall_product = max(max_overall_product, dp_max_prod[i])\n\n    return max_overall_product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "def validate_inputs(list1: list, list2: list) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Validate that inputs are lists containing tuples.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(list1, list) or not isinstance(list2, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Inputs must be lists.\")\n    for item in list1:\n        if not isinstance(item, tuple):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in list1 must be tuples.\")\n    for item in list2:\n        if not isinstance(item, tuple):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in list2 must be tuples.\")\n\ndef normalize_tuple(t: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Convert tuple to normalized form for order-independent comparison.\"\"\"\n    return tuple(sorted(t))\n\ndef find_intersections(list1: list, list2: list) -> set:\n    \"\"\"Find intersections between normalized tuple lists, returning original tuples from list1.\"\"\"\n    normalized_set2 = {normalize_tuple(t) for t in list2}\n    result_set = set()\n    for original_t1 in list1:\n        normalized_t1 = normalize_tuple(original_t1)\n        if normalized_t1 in normalized_set2:\n            result_set.add(original_t1)\n    return result_set\n\ndef tuple_intersection(list1: list, list2: list) -> set:\n    \"\"\"\n    Find intersection of tuples between two lists, ignoring element order.\n\n    Args:\n        list1: First list of tuples\n        list2: Second list of tuples\n\n    Returns:\n        Set of tuples that exist in both lists (order-independent)\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If inputs are not lists or don't contain tuples\n    \"\"\"\n    validate_inputs(list1, list2)\n    return find_intersections(list1, list2)"}
