{"task_id": "Mbpp/567", "solution": "def issort_list(input_list: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Check whether a specified list is sorted in ascending order.\n    Returns True if the list is sorted, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(input_list)\n    if n <= 1:\n        return True  # An empty list or a list with one element is considered sorted.\n\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        if input_list[i] > input_list[i + 1]:\n            return False\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/564", "solution": "def count_Pairs(input_list, length):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of possible unordered pairs where both elements are unequal.\n    Assumes valid input according to design context.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not (isinstance(input_list, list) and all(isinstance(x, int) for x in input_list) and\n            isinstance(length, int) and length >= 0 and len(input_list) == length):\n        # According to the design, _validate_input would handle this,\n        # but since we are not implementing helper functions, we'll\n        # assume valid input as per requirements.\n        # This branch will not be reached given the requirements (\"Do NOT add isinstance() checks or raise exceptions\").\n        # However, for completeness of the design thought process, it's mentioned.\n        pass\n\n    count = 0\n    # Use a set to store seen pairs to handle unordered nature\n    # and avoid double counting (e.g., (1,2) and (2,1)).\n    # We'll generate all combinations and filter later.\n    \n    # The requirement is \"unordered pairs where both elements are unequal\".\n    # This means (1,2) is the same as (2,1) and we don't count (1,1).\n    \n    # Calculate total possible pairs (n * (n-1)) / 2 for unique elements,\n    # then adjust for duplicates.\n    \n    # A more direct approach given the problem statement and example (1,2,1):\n    # For a list [1,2,1], length 3.\n    # Pairs:\n    # (1 at index 0, 2 at index 1) -> unequal (1,2)\n    # (1 at index 0, 1 at index 2) -> equal   (1,1)\n    # (2 at index 1, 1 at index 2) -> unequal (2,1)\n    # Unordered means (1,2) and (2,1) are the same.\n    # So the unique unequal pairs are just (1,2). The count should be 1.\n    # The assert count_Pairs([1,2,1],3) == 2 suggests that\n    # (1_at_idx0, 2_at_idx1) and (2_at_idx1, 1_at_idx2) are considered distinct pairs.\n    # This interpretation implies we are counting pairs of *indices* (i, j) where i < j\n    # and input_list[i] != input_list[j].\n\n    # Iterate through all unique pairs of indices (i, j) where i < j.\n    for i in range(length):\n        for j in range(i + 1, length):\n            if input_list[i] != input_list[j]:\n                count += 1\n    \n    return count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/566", "solution": "def sum_digits(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of the digits of a non-negative integer.\"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    total_sum = 0\n    while n > 0:\n        total_sum += n % 10\n        n //= 10\n    return total_sum"}
